\]

Next, \( 
98 \) isn’t divisible by 2 so increment and move on to the next prime divisor which is 3:
\[
98 \div 3 = (Not divisible)
\]

Now try the next prime number 5:
\(
98 \div 5 \approx (Does not go into 98 exactly.)
\)

Next check for divisibility by 7 since it appears smaller than 98
\[
98 \div 7 = 14
\]

Then calculate \(14\)’s multiplication by 7,
\[
14 \times 7 = 98
\]

Therefore, one set of prime factors in sequence is:

